{:edb/db-before-t 1513 :edb/db-after-t 1514 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534826 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:12:19.504Z" 13194139534826 true] [17592186166501 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2} \\cos(9x)\\cos(x)\\,\\text{d}x$." 13194139534826 false] [17592186166501 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2} \\cos(9x)\\cos(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534826 true] [17592186166501 1145 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (α - β) &= \\cos α\\cos β + \\sin α\\sin β \\\\\n\\cos (α + β) &= \\cos α\\cos β - \\sin α\\sin β\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (α - β) + \\cos (α + β) &= 2\\cos α\\cos β \\\\\n\\cos α\\cos β &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (α - β) + \\cos (α + β))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (9x)\\cos (x) &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (9x - x) + \\cos (9x + x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (8x) + \\cos (10x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{π/4}^{π/2}\\cos (9x)\\cos (x)dx &= \\int_{π/4}^{π/2}\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (8x) + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (10x)dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{16}\\sin (8x) + \\frac{1}{20}\\sin (10x)]_{π/4}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{16}(\\sin (4π) - \\sin (2π)) + \\frac{1}{20}(\\sin (5π) - \\sin (\\frac{5π}{2})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{16}(0 - 0) + \\frac{1}{20}(0 - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534826 false] [17592186166501 1145 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x-x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{20}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534826 true] [17592186166502 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(12x)\\sin(5x)\\,\\text{d}x .$" 13194139534826 true] [17592186166502 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(12x)\\sin(5x)\\,\\text{d}x$." 13194139534826 false] [17592186166502 1145 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (α - β) &= \\cos α\\cos β + \\sin α\\sin β \\\\\n\\cos (α + β) &= \\cos α\\cos β - \\sin α\\sin β\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (α - β) - \\cos (α + β) &= 2\\sin α\\sin β \\\\\n\\sin α\\sin β &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (α - β) - \\cos (α + β))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (12x)\\sin (5x) &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (12x - 5x) - \\cos (12x + 5x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (7x) - \\cos (17x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sin (12x)\\sin (5x)dx &= \\int\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (7x) - \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (17x)dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{14}\\sin (7x) - \\frac{1}{34}\\sin (17x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534826 false] [17592186166502 1145 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(17x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(17x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{34}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(17x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139534826 true] [17592186166503 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\sin(2x)\\cos(4x)\\,\\text{d}x$." 13194139534826 false] [17592186166503 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2} \\sin(2x)\\cos(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534826 true] [17592186166503 1145 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (α + β) &= \\sin α\\cos β + \\cos α\\sin β \\\\\n\\sin (α - β) &= \\sin α\\cos β - \\cos α\\sin β\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (α + β) + \\sin (α - β) &= 2\\sin α\\cos β \\\\\n\\sin α\\cos β &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin (α + β) + \\sin (α - β))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (2x)\\cos (4x) &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin (2x + 4x) + \\sin (2x - 4x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin (6x) + \\sin (-2x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin (6x) - \\sin (2x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/2}\\sin (2x)\\cos (4x)dx &= \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (6x) - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{1}{12}\\cos (6x) + \\frac{1}{4}\\cos (2x)]_{0}^{π/2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{12}(\\cos (3π) - \\cos (0)) + \\frac{1}{4}(\\cos (π) - \\cos (0)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{12}(-1 - 1) + \\frac{1}{4}(-1 - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534826 false] [17592186166503 1145 "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(-1-1)+\\frac{1}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534826 true] [17592186166884 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312689 13194139534826 true] [17592186166884 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312696 13194139534826 true] [17592186166884 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312703 13194139534826 true] [17592186166884 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312710 13194139534826 true] [17592186166884 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312731 13194139534826 true] [17592186166885 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312717 13194139534826 true] [17592186166885 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312724 13194139534826 true] [17592186166885 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312735 13194139534826 true] [17592186166885 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312738 13194139534826 true] [17592186166886 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312741 13194139534826 true] [17592186166886 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312748 13194139534826 tru